---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/marketing.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security"
  description="Encrypted pairing, Keychain keys, scoped shares, and revoke — local by design."
>
  <section class="section" style="padding-top:calc(var(--nav-h) + 3rem)">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:720px">
      <p class="eyebrow">Security</p>
      <h1 class="h2">Local by design</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Tether’s threat model is another device on the network trying to read files or impersonate a paired
        phone. There is no account and no cloud sync on the core path.
      </p>

      <div class="sec-blocks">
        <section>
          <h2>Pairing and keys</h2>
          <p>
            A one-time secret rides in the QR (or a shorter typed code). The Mac must approve before a device
            key is created. That key lives in the Keychain on both sides; the one-time secret is discarded.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Encrypted sessions</h2>
          <p>
            Sessions use ChaChaPoly with keys derived via HKDF-SHA256. Handshakes are authenticated with
            HMAC-SHA256. CryptoKit only — no third-party crypto.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Scoped paths</h2>
          <p>
            Every phone path is checked against shared folders before it touches disk. Escapes with
            <code>..</code>, absolute host paths, and symlink tricks are rejected.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Approve and revoke</h2>
          <p>
            You choose which phones connect. Revoke deletes that phone’s key so the next session fails.
            Failed handshakes and pairing attempts are rate-limited.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Agents and boundaries</h2>
          <p>
            The file browser stays inside shares. Allowlisted coding agents run as your Mac user via a live
            PTY — pair only phones you trust. There is still no general interactive shell for arbitrary
            programs.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="privacy">
          <h2>Privacy</h2>
          <p>
            No account. Logs mark paths private and do not record keys, pairing secrets, or file contents.
            A relay, if you use one, forwards ciphertext after the handshake; it does not hold the device key.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="inline-links" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/security">Security deep dive</a>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/docs/remote">Other networks</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .sec-blocks {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  .sec-blocks h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.45rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .sec-blocks p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
  }

  .sec-blocks code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
